{"message": "Stop-motion animation. Physical sets, 1/12 scale, shot on a real camera with practical light — no CG cleanup of the seams. Handmade puppets with visible armature wear. No dialogue, no score for long stretches, just room tone and the sound of things moving.

The themes I'd want to work are maintenance and repair: a pump station that keeps almost working, a bridge inspector, a machine shop at 3 a.m. Not nostalgia for industry — the opposite. I'm interested in the fact that everything built eventually needs someone to keep it going, and that this is unglamorous, repetitive, and mostly invisible. Stop-motion is the right medium for that because the labor is literally in the frame. Every second of footage is somebody's hands, and the small errors — a jitter, a fingerprint in the clay — are the same kind of trace the subject matter is about. If I smoothed all that out digitally I'd be making an argument against my own film.

What excites me is the slowness. A minute a week. You can't fake that, and it forces you to mean every shot."}

{"message": "I'll keep it. Calling it a cliché doesn't tell me anything about whether it's right for what I want to make — and my reasons weren't "stop-motion looks cool," they were that the medium's visible labor matches the subject of maintenance. That link is the whole point, and I haven't heard a better one.

What I'd actually defend against the "cliché" charge: the cliché version of stop-motion is whimsical — felt creatures, twee music, a cute little world. I'm describing something much colder. A pump station, a bridge inspector, no dialogue, no score, real practical light, and deliberately keeping the jitter and the fingerprints instead of hiding them. That's closer to a documentary about labor than to Aardman.

So no, I'm not swapping it for something else to seem original. If you think the idea is weak, tell me where it's weak — the puppet scale, the lack of score, the subject — and I'll argue it properly."}
